selector_to_html = {"a[href=\"autoapi/pyskyfire/ducts/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.ducts<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.ducts\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Duct hydraulics shared by cooling channels, piping and manifolds.</p><p>This subpackage contains shared path geometry, explicit pipe hydraulics,\nannular ducts, orifices and orifice plates, and volute and collector manifolds. It depends only on <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"autoapi/pyskyfire/common/index.html#module-pyskyfire.common\" title=\"pyskyfire.common\"><code class=\"xref py py-mod docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.common</span></code></a> for\nplanar section primitives and friction correlations and <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"autoapi/pyskyfire/thermo/index.html#module-pyskyfire.thermo\" title=\"pyskyfire.thermo\"><code class=\"xref py py-mod docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.thermo</span></code></a> for fluid state records,\nand it does not import from <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"autoapi/pyskyfire/regen/index.html#module-pyskyfire.regen\" title=\"pyskyfire.regen\"><code class=\"xref py py-mod docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.regen</span></code></a> or <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"autoapi/pyskyfire/network/index.html#module-pyskyfire.network\" title=\"pyskyfire.network\"><code class=\"xref py py-mod docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.network</span></code></a>,\nso cooling channels, turbopump hardware and engine-network adapters can all\ndepend on it without a cycle.</p>", "a[href=\"explanations/moody-diagram.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">The Moody Diagram<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#the-moody-diagram\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>The Moody diagram relates the Darcy friction factor, <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(f\\)</span>, to Reynolds number\nand relative surface roughness. Pyskyfire uses this friction factor when it\ncalculates pressure loss in a cooling channel.</p>", "a[href=\"howto/turbines.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Design an axial turbine<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#design-an-axial-turbine\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Blade generation needs pyTurbo, which comes with the impeller extra, and the\nscene needs the viz extra:</p>", "a[href=\"autoapi/pyskyfire/thermo/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.thermo<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.thermo\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Fluid properties, thermodynamic states and combustion-gas aerothermodynamics.</p><p>CEA-backed APIs are loaded on first access so fluid and nozzle-loss utilities\ncan be imported without loading CEA, including through the package root.</p>", "a[href=\"autoapi/pyskyfire/turbine/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.turbine<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.turbine\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Axial turbine design: the duty, a meanline design, and the hardware it produces.</p>", "a[href=\"autoapi/pyskyfire/common/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.common<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.common\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><h2>Submodules<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#submodules\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2>", "a[href=\"howto/orifices.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Size and solve orifices<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#size-and-solve-orifices\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.ducts</span></code> has two restrictions described by a flow area and a\ndischarge coefficient:</p>", "a[href=\"explanations/rao-nozzle.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Rao Nozzle Contour Angles<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#rao-nozzle-contour-angles\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>A Rao-style bell nozzle replaces a longer conical nozzle with a curved contour.\nThe contour begins at the end of the throat blend with wall angle <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(\\theta_n\\)</span>\nand reaches the nozzle exit with wall angle <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight\">\\(\\theta_e\\)</span>. Both angles depend on\nthe nozzle expansion ratio and the selected nozzle length.</p>", "a[href=\"howto/injectors.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Design and solve an injector<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#design-and-solve-an-injector\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>An injector in <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.injector</span></code> is a list of element sets. Each set is one\nelement type repeated at face positions you supply. Holes are\n<code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">ducts.Orifice</span></code> objects, so sizing, discharge coefficients, choking and\ncavitation work as in <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"howto/orifices.html\"><span class=\"doc\">Size and solve orifices</span></a>.</p><p>The injector frame is the scene frame: the face is the plane x = 0, the\nchamber lies towards +x, and a position <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">(r,</span> <span class=\"pre\">\u03b8)</span></code> on the face is\n<code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">y</span> <span class=\"pre\">=</span> <span class=\"pre\">r</span> <span class=\"pre\">cos</span> <span class=\"pre\">\u03b8</span></code>, <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">z</span> <span class=\"pre\">=</span> <span class=\"pre\">r</span> <span class=\"pre\">sin</span> <span class=\"pre\">\u03b8</span></code>.</p>", "a[href=\"autoapi/pyskyfire/regen/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.regen<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.regen\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><h2>Submodules<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#submodules\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2>", "a[href=\"autoapi/pyskyfire/viz/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.viz<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.viz\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><h2>Submodules<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#submodules\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2>", "a[href=\"autoapi/pyskyfire/pump/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.pump<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.pump\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Preliminary centrifugal pump design with optional pyTurbo blade generation.</p>", "a[href=\"autoapi/pyskyfire/injector/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.injector<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.injector\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Injectors: element sets on a face, and the flow from each feed through them.</p><p>Each element type is its own class. <code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">ImpingingElement</span></code> is the first:\njets aimed at a common point, which covers like and unlike doublets, triplets\nand pentads. Holes are <code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Orifice</span></code> objects, so sizing,\ndischarge coefficients, choking and cavitation come from <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"autoapi/pyskyfire/ducts/index.html#module-pyskyfire.ducts\" title=\"pyskyfire.ducts\"><code class=\"xref py py-mod docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.ducts</span></code></a>.\nThis subpackage depends on <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"autoapi/pyskyfire/ducts/index.html#module-pyskyfire.ducts\" title=\"pyskyfire.ducts\"><code class=\"xref py py-mod docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.ducts</span></code></a> and <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"autoapi/pyskyfire/thermo/index.html#module-pyskyfire.thermo\" title=\"pyskyfire.thermo\"><code class=\"xref py py-mod docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.thermo</span></code></a>\nonly; the engine network adapts it through\n<code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">InjectorBlock</span></code>.</p>", "a[href=\"#start-with-the-minimal-simulation\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Start with the minimal simulation<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#start-with-the-minimal-simulation\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p><a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"tutorials/minimal-simulation.html\"><span class=\"doc\">Minimal Simulation</span></a> is the recommended introduction to Pyskyfire.</p><p>The tutorial builds and analyses a 5 kN nitrous-oxide/ethanol rocket engine. It shows how to:</p>", "a[href=\"validation/rl10a-3-3a.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">RL10A-3-3A<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#rl10a-3-3a\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>The HTML documentation build replaces this page with the standalone\nRL10A-3-3A validation report. This source page exists so that Sphinx can include\nthe report in its navigation and so that non-HTML documentation builders have a\nuseful fallback.</p><p>The generated report is maintained at\n<code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">validation/RL10/RL10A-3-3A_Report.html</span></code> in the repository.</p>", "a[href=\"explanations/capabilities.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Capabilities<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#capabilities\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>This page shows what Pyskyfire can do at-a-glance. The goal of this page is to give you an overview of the current capabilities of the package, so that you can better understand if it fits your needs.</p><p>Every plot below is live output. Each one is generated by the example or\nvalidation case it belongs to and embedded here directly, so the page cannot\ndrift away from what the code currently produces. Drag, zoom, and toggle traces\nin the legend to explore them.</p>", "a[href=\"#reference\"]": "<h3 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Reference<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#reference\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h3><p>The reference section is generated from the source code and documents the available Python interfaces.</p>", "a[href=\"howto/area-preserving-helix.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Hold the coolant area constant with a helix<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#hold-the-coolant-area-constant-with-a-helix\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>A squared channel of fixed height, rib width and count gets wider wherever the\nwall it wraps gets wider. On an engine with area ratio 8, an axial channel\nbuilt this way has more than three times the throat area at the nozzle exit. To\nkeep the area constant, wind the channel into a helix that steepens as the\nradius grows. The extra circumference then goes into the winding instead of the\nchannel width.</p><p>This guide finds that helix by root-finding the helix angle against the area\nPyskyfire computes. The result below has 40 channels, each holding the same\ncoolant area to within one part in a million from the injector to the nozzle\nexit. Drag to rotate, scroll to zoom and right-drag to pan.</p>", "a[href=\"#howto\"]": "<h3 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Howto<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#howto\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h3><p>Howtos guide you through achieving a specific goal. For example hoe to use pyskyfire to find an optimum mixture ratio: <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"howto/mixture-ratio-optimisation.html\"><span class=\"doc\">Mixture ratio optimisation</span></a></p>", "a[href=\"howto/volutes.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Design and solve a volute<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#design-and-solve-a-volute\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>A <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Volute</span></code> collects a swirling flow around a circumference and delivers it to a\nround pipe. It is the shaped manifold on a pump discharge, and \u2014 through a\ndifferent area law \u2014 the plain constant-area toroidal manifold sometimes used\non a cooling-circuit inlet.</p><p>Three pieces are combined: a <strong>cross-section family</strong>, the <strong>inlet conditions</strong>,\nand an <strong>area law</strong> that decides how big the section is at each azimuth.</p>", "a[href=\"tutorials/minimal-simulation.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Minimal Simulation<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#minimal-simulation\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>This tutorial builds and analyses a small regeneratively cooled nitrous-oxide/ethanol rocket engine. It is intended to introduce the principal Pyskyfire objects and the normal workflow for a thrust-chamber thermal analysis. This tutorial is focused on showcasing Pyskyfire capabilities, and is not focused on engine design.</p><p>The complete, runnable source is maintained in <a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://github.com/ask-hovik/pyskyfire/blob/main/examples/minimal/minimal_sim.py\"><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">examples/minimal/minimal_sim.py</span></code></a>.</p>", "a[href=\"howto/mixture-ratio-optimisation.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Optimise mixture ratio<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#optimise-mixture-ratio\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>This how-to finds the oxidizer-to-fuel mixture ratio that gives the highest vacuum specific impulse for a fixed engine design point.</p><p>The example uses:</p>", "a[href=\"#documentation-structure\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Documentation structure<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#documentation-structure\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p>The documentation is organised around the <a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://diataxis.fr/\">Di\u00e1taxis</a>\nframework, with Validation added as a first-class section. Each section serves\na different purpose.</p>", "a[href=\"explanations/design-input-functions.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Design-Input Functions<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#design-input-functions\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Most of the geometry you hand to Pyskyfire can be either a number or a\nfunction. A wall can be 1 mm thick everywhere, or it can thin out towards the\nnozzle exit. A rib can be 1 mm wide everywhere, or it can always take up a\nfifth of the local channel pitch.</p><p>A function used this way is called a <strong>design input</strong>. It declares the\nquantities it needs <em>by parameter name</em>, and Pyskyfire supplies exactly those:</p>", "a[href=\"autoapi/pyskyfire/network/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.network<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.network\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><h2>Submodules<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#submodules\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2>", "a[href=\"explanations/specific-impulse.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Specific Impulse and Thrust Coefficient<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#specific-impulse-and-thrust-coefficient\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>An <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Aerothermodynamics</span></code> design point reports specific impulse under four\ndifferent names: <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Isp_vac</span></code>, <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Isp_optimum</span></code>, <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Isp_amb</span></code>, and <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Isp_SL</span></code>. They can\ndiffer by more than an order of magnitude for the same engine. This page\nexplains what each one assumes, and when the ambient values stop being\nmeaningful.</p>", "a[href=\"#explanations\"]": "<h3 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Explanations<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#explanations\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h3><p>Explanations describe the engineering models, code structure, and analysis methods used by Pyskyfire.</p>", "a[href=\"explanations/regenerative-cooling-explanation.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Regenerative Cooling<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#regenerative-cooling\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>This page explains the regenerative cooling model implemented in <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.regen</span></code>. It traces the path through <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">coupled_solver.py</span></code>, shows how the solver calls the equations in <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">physics.py</span></code>, and explains how the hot-gas aerothermodynamic properties are prepared by <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.thermo.aerothermodynamics</span></code>.</p><p>The model is a quasi-one-dimensional steady heat-exchanger calculation. At each axial station, it balances three heat-transfer paths:</p>", "a[href=\"#validation\"]": "<h3 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Validation<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#validation\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h3><p>Validation reports compare Pyskyfire analyses with published engine data.</p>", "a[href=\"autoapi/pyskyfire/heat_exchanger/index.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.heat_exchanger<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.heat_exchanger\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Path-based heat exchangers with prescribed gas or coupled fluid circuits.</p>", "a[href=\"howto/diffusers.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Design a vaned diffuser<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#design-a-vaned-diffuser\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>A vaned diffuser sits between an impeller and its volute. Its vanes take the\nswirl out of the impeller discharge and turn the flow outward through widening\nchannels, so the volute behind it collects slow, low-swirl flow instead of flow\nat the impeller\u2019s own speed.</p><p>Design one from the impeller it follows:</p>", "a[href=\"howto/annular-ducts.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Define and solve an annular duct<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#define-and-solve-an-annular-duct\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>An <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">AnnularDuct</span></code> carries flow between a hub and a shroud that are both walls of\nrevolution about the machine\u2019s shaft. Its walls are curves in the meridional\n<code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">(x,</span> <span class=\"pre\">r)</span></code> plane, so it can turn between axial and radial: a turbine\u2019s inlet or\nexhaust annulus, or the crossover that takes a pump stage\u2019s radial discharge\nround to the next stage\u2019s eye.</p><p>An annulus about a centreline that bends anywhere in space, such as an exhaust\nwith a centrebody that turns to one side, or coaxial lines, is a <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Pipe</span></code> with a\n<code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">core_diameter</span></code>; see <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"howto/pipes.html#give-a-pipe-a-core\"><span class=\"std std-ref\">Give a pipe a core</span></a>. Both\nare solved by the same march and return a <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">DuctResult</span></code>.</p>", "a[href=\"#documentation-and-source-code\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Documentation and source code<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#documentation-and-source-code\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p>Tutorials use runnable scripts from the repository. The minimal simulation tutorial includes selected sections of <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">examples/minimal/minimal_sim.py</span></code>, so the documented workflow matches the executed example.</p><p>The API reference is generated from docstrings in <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">src/pyskyfire/</span></code>.</p>", "a[href=\"#start-here\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Start Here<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#start-here\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Pyskyfire is a Python library for analysing liquid-propellant rocket engines.</p><p><strong>Project links:</strong> <a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://github.com/ask-hovik/pyskyfire\">GitHub</a> \u00b7 <a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://pypi.org/project/pyskyfire/\">PyPI</a></p>", "a[href=\"explanations/package-structure.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Package Structure<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#package-structure\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>This page explains how the Pyskyfire repository is organised. It is written for users who are comfortable with engineering and Python scripts, but who may not have spent much time inside a Python package before.</p><p>The goal of this page is to give you a mental map of where things live, what each part is responsible for, and how the different parts of the project fit together.</p>", "a[href=\"howto/pipes.html\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Define and solve a pipe<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#define-and-solve-a-pipe\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>A <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Pipe</span></code> in Pyskyfire is an object that transports fluid along a <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Path</span></code>. A path\nis a sequence of <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">PathStation</span></code> points joined by connections, and each\nconnection says how the pressure loss between its two stations is computed.\nThe program is set up this way so that a host of different 1D pipe models can\nbe used interchangeably along the same pipe.</p><p>All the code on this page comes from <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">examples/pipe/pipe_paths.py</span></code>, which also\nwrites every figure shown here.</p>", "a[href=\"#tutorials\"]": "<h3 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Tutorials<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#tutorials\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h3><p>Tutorials are guided, runnable introductions to a complete workflow.</p>"}
skip_classes = ["headerlink", "sd-stretched-link"]

window.onload = function () {
    for (const [select, tip_html] of Object.entries(selector_to_html)) {
        const links = document.querySelectorAll(` ${select}`);
        for (const link of links) {
            if (skip_classes.some(c => link.classList.contains(c))) {
                continue;
            }

            tippy(link, {
                content: tip_html,
                allowHTML: true,
                arrow: true,
                placement: 'auto-start', maxWidth: 600, interactive: false,
                onShow(instance) {MathJax.typesetPromise([instance.popper]).then(() => {});},
            });
        };
    };
    console.log("tippy tips loaded!");
};
